# 6. PATIENT REGISTRATION AND OPD TOKEN GENERATION

This is one of the most important features.

When the patient clicks Generate OPD Token, open a registration form.

### Patient information

Required fields:

- Patient Name.
- Patient Age.
- Gender, if required for registration.
- Mobile Number.
- Department / Medical Category.
- Cause of Visit.
- Symptoms or Brief Description.
- Previous Visit Information, if available.

### Medical categories

Initially support:

- Orthopaedics.
- Cardiology.
- Neurology.
- Gynaecology.
- General Medicine.
- General Surgery.
- Neurosurgery.
- Physiotherapy.
- Paediatrics.
- ENT.
- Dermatology.

Allow administrators to add more categories.

### Visit information

Capture:

- Date of Visit.
- Preferred OPD date.
- Previous consultation date.
- Time of accident, if applicable.
- Time of registration.
- Visit type: New / Follow-up.
- Emergency or Normal.

Do not treat the time of accident as the appointment time.

### Token generation

After successful registration:

1. Validate patient information.
2. Identify the selected department.
3. Check doctor availability.
4. Identify the applicable OPD schedule.
5. Allocate an appropriate doctor or doctor team.
6. Generate a unique token.
7. Store token information in PostgreSQL.
8. Maintain live queue state in Redis.
9. Broadcast the token information using WebSocket.

### Token confirmation page

Display:

- Token Number.
- Patient Name.
- Department.
- Doctor Name.
- Doctor ID.
- OPD Building.
- Floor Number.
- Room / Cabin Number.
- Scheduled Appointment Time.
- Current Token Being Served.
- Estimated Waiting Time.
- Queue Position.
- Token Status.

Token statuses:

- Registered.
- Waiting.
- Called.
- In Consultation.
- Completed.
- Cancelled.
- No Show.

The patient must be able to track the token in real time.

Allow cancellation and rescheduling according to configurable hospital policies.

